## Mathematical Background

That specific objectivity holds under the Rasch model can also be shown mathematically [see @debelakIntroductionRaschModel2022, p. 30].

Suppose we want to compare two persons, $n$ and $n^\prime$, on item $i$.
A first idea is to take the difference between their probabilities of answering the item with a 1:

$$P(Y_{ni} = 1) - P(Y_{n^\prime i} = 1) = \frac{\exp(\theta_n - \beta_i)}{1 + \exp(\theta_n - \beta_i)} - \frac{\exp(\theta_{n^\prime} - \beta_i)}{1 + \exp(\theta_{n^\prime} - \beta_i)}$$

In this difference, the item difficulty $\beta_i$ does not drop out, so the comparison still depends on the item.
This changes when we compare the persons in log odds instead of probabilities.
If we apply the link function to the probability of answering an item with a 1, we obtain the log odds on the left-hand side and only the linear term $\theta_n - \beta_i$ on the right-hand side:

$$\log\left(\frac{P(Y_{ni} = 1)}{1 - P(Y_{ni} = 1)}\right) = \theta_n - \beta_i$$

The difference between the two persons in log odds is then:

$$\begin{aligned}
\log\left(\frac{P(Y_{ni} = 1)}{1 - P(Y_{ni} = 1)}\right) - \log\left(\frac{P(Y_{n^\prime i} = 1)}{1 - P(Y_{n^\prime i} = 1)}\right) &= (\theta_n - \beta_i) - (\theta_{n^\prime} - \beta_i)\\
&= \theta_n - \beta_i - \theta_{n^\prime} +  \beta_i\\
&= \theta_n - \theta_{n^\prime} +  \beta_i - \beta_i\\
&= \theta_n - \theta_{n^\prime}
\end{aligned}$$

Only the difference between the two $\theta$ values remains; the item difficulty cancels out.
When we compare two persons, it therefore does not matter which item we use.

The same can be shown for the comparison of two items, $i$ and $i^\prime$:

$$\begin{aligned}
\log\left(\frac{P(Y_{ni} = 1)}{1 - P(Y_{ni} = 1)}\right) - \log\left(\frac{P(Y_{ni^\prime} = 1)}{1 - P(Y_{ni^\prime} = 1)}\right) &= (\theta_n - \beta_i) - (\theta_{n} - \beta_{i^\prime})\\
&= \theta_n - \beta_i - \theta_n +  \beta_{i^\prime}\\
&= \theta_n - \theta_n + \beta_{i^\prime} - \beta_i\\
&= \beta_{i^\prime} - \beta_i
\end{aligned}$$

Here the person parameter cancels out.
When we compare two items, it therefore does not matter which person answered them.
Both results together are what specific objectivity means.
:::

Specific objectivity can be seen as a form of fairness: the comparison of two persons rests on $\theta$ alone and does not depend on the items used [@debelakIntroductionRaschModel2022, p. 28].
For scale development, it is equally important that the comparison of items does not depend on who answers them [see @raschGeneralLawsMeaning1961, p. 332; @wilsonConstructingMeasuresItem2023, p. 248].


### Identifiability {#sec-identi-Rasch}

This tutorial does not cover in detail how the parameters of a Rasch model are estimated.
One point matters here, however: the Rasch model has an identifiability problem.
It arises because the scale on which we locate $\theta$ and $\beta$ is a latent scale.
A latent variable cannot be observed directly, so its scale has no natural origin [see also @debelakIntroductionRaschModel2022, for an illustration of this point].

Where we place the origin is therefore arbitrary, as long as we use the same origin for $\theta$ and $\beta$ [@lordApplicationsItemResponse1980].
As we have already seen, the probability that a person answers an item with a 1 depends only on the difference between $\theta_n$ and $\beta_i$.
We can thus shift all $\theta$ and $\beta$ values along the scale by the same amount without changing any response probability.
A person with $\theta = 4$ has the same probability of answering an item with $\beta = 2$ with a 1 ($4 - 2 = 2$) as a person with $\theta = 6$ has for an item with $\beta = 4$ ($6 - 4 = 2$), and the same holds for every other combination with a difference of 2.
For the estimation, this means that the data cannot single out one solution: infinitely many sets of $\theta$ and $\beta$ values fit the data equally well.
This is called the identifiability problem, and the model is said to be not identified [see also @deayalaTheoryPracticeItem2022, p. 44].

::: {.callout-tip appearance="simple"}
## Mathematical Background

**Additive Indeterminacy** [@debelakIntroductionRaschModel2022; @lordApplicationsItemResponse1980]

Suppose we add the same constant $c$ to every $\theta_n$ and every $\beta_i$, which gives us the new parameters $\theta_n'$ and $\beta_i'$:

$$\begin{aligned}
\theta_n' &= \theta_n + c\\
\beta_i' &= \beta_i + c
\end{aligned}$$

We can now compare the response probabilities under the two sets of parameters.
To keep the notation simple, we use the log odds instead of the probability, so that only a linear term remains on the right-hand side:

$$\begin{aligned}
\log\left(\frac{P(Y_{ni} = 1\mid\theta_n, \beta_i)}{P(Y_{ni} = 0\mid\theta_n, \beta_i)}\right) &= \theta_n - \beta_i\\
\log\left(\frac{P(Y_{ni} = 1\mid\theta_n', \beta_i')}{P(Y_{ni} = 0\mid\theta_n', \beta_i')}\right) &= \theta_n' - \beta_i'
\end{aligned}$$

If the origin of the scale is arbitrary, both sets of parameters must give the same log odds.
We can check this by inserting the definitions of $\theta_n'$ and $\beta_i'$:

$$\begin{aligned}
\log\left(\frac{P(Y_{ni} = 1\mid\theta_n', \beta_i')}{P(Y_{ni} = 0\mid\theta_n', \beta_i')}\right) &= \theta_n' - \beta_i'\\
&= (\theta_n + c) - (\beta_i + c)\\
&= \theta_n + c - \beta_i - c\\
&= \theta_n - \beta_i + c - c\\
&= \theta_n - \beta_i\\
&= \log\left(\frac{P(Y_{ni} = 1\mid\theta_n, \beta_i)}{P(Y_{ni} = 0\mid\theta_n, \beta_i)}\right)
\end{aligned}$$

The constant cancels out, and we are left with $\theta_n - \beta_i$, the log odds under the original parameters.
Both sets of parameters therefore give the same response probabilities for every person and every item, whatever the value of $c$.
The data cannot tell them apart, which is why the model is not identified.
:::

To solve this problem, we have to give the latent scale a fixed origin and a fixed unit.
The unit is already fixed in the Rasch model, because the $\alpha$ parameter is set to 1 ([crossreference here to 1PL section]{.mark}), so we only need to fix the origin [@deayalaTheoryPracticeItem2022, p. 44].
There are several ways to do this [@deayalaTheoryPracticeItem2022; @lordApplicationsItemResponse1980].
They are called restrictions, because they restrict the possible solutions until only one is left.

- **Sum norm restriction (item centering):** We add a rule to the estimation: the item difficulties have to sum to 0 ($\sum_{i = 1}^I \beta_i = 0$).
  The estimation still looks for the parameters under which the observed data are most likely, but among all the equally good solutions only one satisfies the rule, and the model is identified.

- **Fixing the difficulty of one item (anchor item):** We set the difficulty $\beta_i$ of one item to a specific value, usually 0.
  Since the data determine the distances between the items, fixing one item fixes all the others.
  Suppose that Item 2 is two units more difficult than Item 1, and Item 3 is one unit easier than Item 1.
  Without a restriction, infinitely many sets of values represent these distances, for example $\beta_1 = 2$, $\beta_2 = 4$, and $\beta_3 = 1$, or $\beta_1 = 10$, $\beta_2 = 12$, and $\beta_3 = 9$.
  If we set $\beta_1 = 0$, only one solution remains: $\beta_2 = 2$ and $\beta_3 = -1$.

- **Setting the mean of $\theta$ to 0 (person centering):** This works like the sum norm restriction, but on the person side: the mean of $\theta$ has to be 0.
  This is the restriction mirt uses: it assumes that $\theta$ is normally distributed and fixes the mean of this distribution at 0.

Since $\theta$ and $\beta$ lie on the same latent scale, one of these restrictions is enough to fix the origin [@deayalaTheoryPracticeItem2022, p. 44].
Which one we choose does not change the response probabilities or the distances between the parameters; it only determines where the zero point lies.

