The ICC shows $\theta$ on the x-axis and the probability of answering the item with a 1 on the y-axis.
The curve is S-shaped, so its slope is not constant, as it would be for a straight line, but changes along the x-axis.
The same difference in $\theta$ therefore changes the response probability by different amounts, depending on where on the continuum we are.

For persons with very high (or very low) $\theta$ values, the probability hardly changes when $\theta$ increases or decreases.
Persons C and D in @fig-example_ICC_info have rather high $\theta$ values ($\theta_C = 4$ and $\theta_D = 5$).
Although they differ by one unit of $\theta$, their probabilities of answering the item with a 1 are almost the same ($P(Y_{Ci} = 1) = 0.98$ and $P(Y_{Di} = 1) = 0.99$).
This is because the ICC is flat in the low and high $\theta$ ranges: changes along the x-axis are hardly reflected on the y-axis.

The situation is different around the item difficulty (here, $\beta_i = 0$), where the ICC is steep.
Persons A and B ($\theta_A = -0.5$ and $\theta_B = 0.5$) also differ by exactly one unit of $\theta$.
Here, however, the difference matters a great deal: Person A has a probability of 38% of answering the item with a 1, and Person B a probability of 62%.
Put simply, Person A is unlikely to agree with the item, while Person B is likely to agree with it.

The steeper the slope, then, the more a difference in $\theta$ shows up as a difference in the response probability, and the better the item distinguishes between persons, even if their $\theta$ values are close together.
We "learn" the most about persons' $\theta$ values in those ranges of the continuum where the ICC is steep.
In other words, the item provides the most *information* there.
Item information thus tells us which range of $\theta$ an item measures especially well.

Item information is therefore tied to the slope of the ICC.
The ICC is steepest at its inflection point, and we have learned that the inflection point lies at the item difficulty $\beta$.
Most of an item's information is accordingly concentrated around its difficulty.

::: {.callout-tip appearance="simple"}
## Mathematical Background

In the Rasch model, the information of item $i$ at a given value $\theta_n$, written $I_i(\theta_n)$, is the probability of answering the item with a 1 times the probability of answering it with a 0 [@debelakIntroductionRaschModel2022, p. 51; @fischerRaschModelsFoundations1995, p. 55]:

$$\begin{align}
I_i(\theta_n) &= P(Y_{ni} = 1\mid\theta_n,\beta_i) \cdot P(Y_{ni} = 0 \mid \theta_n,\beta_i)\\
&= \frac{\exp(\theta_n - \beta_i)}{1 + \exp(\theta_n - \beta_i)} \cdot \frac{1}{1 + \exp(\theta_n - \beta_i)}
\end{align}$$ {#eq-iteminfo}

This product is also the slope of the ICC at $\theta_n$, which is why the information is high where the ICC is steep.
:::

If we compute the information $I_i(\theta_n)$ for every possible value of $\theta$ (see @eq-iteminfo) and plot $\theta$ on the x-axis and the information on the y-axis, we obtain the *item information curve* (see @fig-example_item_info).

::: {#fig-example_item_info}
