:::

The curve shows at a glance where on the $\theta$ continuum the item is most informative, that is, which range it measures most precisely.
We have seen that the information is greatest at the item difficulty, because the ICC is steepest there.
We also know that the probability of answering with a 1 is 50% at the item difficulty, because this is how the item difficulty is defined.
The largest possible information of an item in the Rasch model is therefore 0.25 ($I_i(\theta_n = \beta_i) = 0.5 \cdot 0.5 = 0.25$), and the peak of the item information curve always lies at the item difficulty.

That precision depends on $\theta$ is one advantage of IRT over CTT.
CTT expresses precision as reliability, a single value that applies to all persons alike.
This implies that an item measures equally well at every level, which is rather unrealistic.
Consider an "easy" item from the ECo scale.
Among persons with a low $\theta$, some will answer it with a 1 and others will not, so the item differentiates between them.
Persons with a high $\theta$, in contrast, will almost all answer it with a 1, so the item tells us next to nothing about the differences between them.
Reliability in CTT would nevertheless suggest that the item measures both groups equally well.
Item information gives a more realistic picture, since each item measures only a certain range of $\theta$ precisely.
Put differently, in IRT reliability varies over $\theta$ [@hoffmanLectureNotesFactor2024].

#### Test Information

Most of the time, we do not work with a single item but with a whole scale.
We have learned that the information of an item is largest around its difficulty, and difficulties vary from item to item.
This raises the question of how well the scale as a whole differentiates between persons across the $\theta$ continuum.
The test information answers this question.

Since each item contributes some information, the test information is the sum of the item information of all items [@deayalaTheoryPracticeItem2022, p. 31]:

$$I(\theta) = \sum_{i=1}^I I_i(\theta)$$ {#eq-testinfo}

If we compute the test information for every possible value of $\theta$ with @eq-testinfo and plot $\theta$ on the x-axis and the test information on the y-axis, we obtain the *test information curve* (see @fig-example_ICC_test_info for an example).

