Test information of a hypothetical scale and the standard error of measurement

:::

Information is a measure of precision, so it is directly related to the standard error of $\theta$, a measure of imprecision [@hoffmanLectureNotesFactor2024; @meiserBB2NeueEntwicklungen2025; @deayalaTheoryPracticeItem2022, p. 31]:

$$\mathit{SE}(\theta) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{I(\theta)}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^I I_i(\theta)}}$$

This makes intuitive sense, as @fig-example_ICC_test_info shows: where the test information is high, the standard error is small, and vice versa.

The test information reveals another difference to CTT.
In CTT, how much an item adds to the reliability of a scale depends on the other items in the scale, namely on how strongly the item correlates with them [@lordApplicationsItemResponse1980, p. 22].
In IRT, the information an item contributes does not depend on the other items. This holds only, however, under local independence since locally dependent items share part of their information, and the sum then overstates the precision of the scale.

Despite this difference, the test information can be converted into a reliability [@hoffmanLectureNotesFactor2024, p. 41]:

$$\text{Reliability}(\theta) = \frac{I(\theta)}{I(\theta) + 1/\sigma^2_\theta}$$

Here, $\sigma^2_\theta$ is the variance of $\theta$; if it is fixed at 1, the formula simplifies to $I(\theta)/(I(\theta) + 1)$.
For a formal derivation, see @lordApplicationsItemResponse1980 [p. 52].
This reliability is again specific to $\theta$, because the test information is.
To obtain a single model-based reliability for the whole scale, one could average the reliabilities over all $\theta$ values, weighted by the number of persons at each value [@hoffmanLectureNotesFactor2024].
@hoffmanLectureNotesFactor2024, however, questions the value of such a summary, since it gives up the very advantage that IRT provides [see also @lordApplicationsItemResponse1980].

#### Practical Use of Information

Item and test information are very useful for diagnosticians and in scale development, because they show at a glance how precisely, and in which ranges of $\theta$, our items or our scale measure [see also @deayalaTheoryPracticeItem2022 for an illustration of this point].
The fictional example in @fig-usecase_info illustrates this.
The scale consists of five items with known item difficulties: $\beta_1 = 0$, $\beta_2 = 1$, $\beta_3 = 3.5$, $\beta_4 = -1.5$, and $\beta_5 = -3.5$.
With these, we can compute and plot the item information and the test information for all values of $\theta$.

