:::

As we have learned, an item differentiates best between persons around its difficulty, and this is where most of its information lies.
Take Item 5, an easy item with a very low $\beta$.
Its information curve peaks at the item difficulty $\beta_5 = -3.5$ and falls off to both sides.
Item 5 is therefore well suited for persons with a low $\theta$ around −3.5, but it tells us hardly anything about persons with high $\theta$ values.
For a person with $\theta_n = 2.5$, for example, its information is practically 0.

For Item 3, the opposite is true.
It is a difficult item ($\beta_3 = 3.5$) and differentiates well between persons with high $\theta$ values.
If we want to measure persons with high $\theta$ values, Item 3 is a better choice than Item 5.
The price is that Item 3 is not suitable for persons with low $\theta$ values, as its flat information curve in the low range shows.

Fortunately, we usually measure with a scale of several items and not with a single item.
We therefore do not need one item that measures the whole range of $\theta$ with high information, which would be unrealistic.
Instead, we can ask what the instrument is meant for.
Suppose we want to develop an instrument for the general population.
For many constructs, $\theta$ is assumed to be normally distributed in the population: most persons have medium $\theta$ values, and some have values well below or above the average.
Our instrument therefore has to cover a broad range of $\theta$.
The five items in @fig-usecase_info suit this purpose, because their difficulties are spread widely and their information curves together cover the whole relevant range (from −5 to 5).
The test information curve summarizes this: the scale provides the most information around $\theta = 0$, and the information decreases to both sides.
The scale is thus most precise where we expect most persons to be.
Keep in mind that this is an idealized example with invented item difficulties.

Now consider another scenario: an instrument designed only for persons with high $\theta$ values, for example an intelligence test for highly gifted persons.
For this purpose, our hypothetical scale is not optimal.
Items 5, 4, and 1 contribute very little in the range we are interested in, and only Item 3 differentiates well there.
The test information curve also drops quite steeply from $\theta = 2.5$ onwards.
We would need more items with a high difficulty to gain information in the high range, and in return we could drop items that are informative only in ranges we are not interested in, such as Items 5, 4, and 1.
As @hoffmanLectureNotesFactor2024 notes, there is therefore no universally "ideal" test information curve: the best curve depends on the purpose of the instrument.

The idea that the items should be spread across the range of $\theta$ we want to measure should be familiar.
We met it with the Wright map in @sec-wright-map, where we compared the distribution of the item difficulties with the histogram of the person parameters.
In doing so, we were already implicitly looking at item information, because most of an item's information is concentrated around its difficulty.
The information curves give a more detailed picture, since they show the whole distribution of the information and not only its maximum at $\beta_i$.

These examples show an advantage of IRT over CTT.
Because item and person parameters lie on the same latent scale and information is defined relative to $\theta$, we get a much more nuanced picture of our instrument and of the persons for whom it works well.
In scale development, we can therefore tailor an instrument to the requirements of the measurement situation [@deayalaTheoryPracticeItem2022, p. 35].

IRT can thus be used to:

1.  **Build or improve scales so that they are informative where we need them**.
  @lordApplicationsItemResponse1980 [p. 23] suggests working with a target information curve, that is, the test information curve we would like the scale to have.
  The target follows from the purpose of the scale: it should be high where we need precise measurement.
  Often this is where the persons we want to measure are located, which we either know from the existing evidence or assume, for example that $\theta$ is normally distributed in the general population, so that the scale should be most informative around the average.
  It can also be a specific point, for example a cut-off above which persons are selected.
  Lord's procedure then works item by item and assumes a pool of items whose information curves are already known.
  We begin with the ranges under the target curve that are hardest to fill, typically those for which only few suitable items are available, and select items whose information lies there.
  After each selection, we add up the information of the items chosen so far and compare this curve with the target.
  We continue, going back and replacing earlier choices if necessary, until the area under the target curve is filled well enough.
  For an existing scale, the items it already has are the starting point.
 The development of the ECo scale shows how information curves are used in this way: the item and test information curves of existing instruments for environmental concern showed little information at high levels of environmental concern, and this gap was one motivation for the new scale [@gommHowMeasureEnvironmental2026].
  In most cases, there is no pool of tested items, and we have to write new ones.
  The construct map can guide us here: it describes what persons at different levels of the construct are expected to agree with, so we can write items aimed at the level where information is missing.
  Whether a new item is as difficult as intended only shows once we have collected data and estimated its difficulty, so we usually write more items than we need and select among them afterwards.


2. **Create short versions of scales**. For example when the information curves of two items overlap considerably.
  We can then omit one of the two items and still cover that range of $\theta$, although with somewhat less information there.

::: {.callout-note appearance="simple"}
## Let's do it in R

To plot item information, we can use the functions we already know from the ICCs: `itemplot()` plots the information curve of a single item, and `plot()` plots the information curves of all items.
The only new element is the value of the `type` argument: instead of `"trace"` for the ICC, we use `"info"` with `itemplot()` and `"infotrace"` with `plot()`.

```{r}
# Item information curve of a single item
itemplot(eco_rasch, item = 6, type = "info")

# Item information curves of all items, each in its own panel
plot(eco_rasch, type = "infotrace", theta_lim = c(-5, 5), facet_items = TRUE)

# Item information curves of all items in a single panel
plot(eco_rasch, type = "infotrace", theta_lim = c(-5, 5), facet_items = FALSE)
```

The test information curve is also plotted with `plot()`, using `type = "info"`:

```{r}
# Test information curve
plot(eco_rasch, type = "info", theta_lim = c(-5, 5))
```

Some values of `type` combine several kinds of information in one plot.
With `plot()`, `"infoSE"` shows the test information together with the standard error.
With `itemplot()`, `"infotrace"` shows the information curve of an item together with its ICC.
Note that `"infotrace"` thus means different things in the two functions: the information curves of all items in `plot()`, but the information curve and the ICC of a single item in `itemplot()`.

```{r}
# Test information curve with standard error
plot(eco_rasch, type = "infoSE", theta_lim = c(-5, 5))

# Information curve and ICC of a single item
itemplot(eco_rasch, item = 6, type = "infotrace")
```
:::

## 2PL

In the Rasch model, we assume that differences between responses can be explained entirely by the difference between the person parameter $\theta$ and the item difficulty $\beta$.
In regression terms, this difference is the only term in the model's linear predictor (see @eq-rasch-logit).

This assumption may be too strict.
Items can also differ in how well they "differentiate among individuals located at different points on the continuum" [@deayalaTheoryPracticeItem2022, p. 137], a property known as *discrimination*.
We already know this idea from the Rasch model, where it is reflected in the slope of the ICC.
In the Rasch model, however, the discrimination is fixed at 1 for all items, so all ICCs run in parallel.
All items therefore differentiate between persons equally strongly; they differ only in where on the $\theta$ continuum they do so.
If we relax this assumption, items can differentiate to different degrees, and their ICCs can cross, as we saw in @fig-ICC_spec_obj_2PL.

The resulting model has two item-specific parameters, the item difficulty and the item discrimination.
Since it extends the one-parameter logistic model (1PL) by a second item parameter, it is called the *two-parameter logistic model* (2PL), sometimes also the Birnbaum model [@citation to birnbaum1968].

### Model Formula

The model equation of the 2PL looks very similar to that of the Rasch model:

$$
P(Y_{ni} = 1) = \frac{\exp(\alpha_i(\theta_n - \beta_i))}{1 + \exp(\alpha_i(\theta_n - \beta_i))}
$$ {#eq-2PL}

Most of its components are already familiar from the Rasch model (see @sec-rasch-formula): the response $Y_{ni}$ of person $n$ to item $i$ is again 0 or 1 and follows a Bernoulli distribution, and the probability of a 1 again depends on the person parameter $\theta_n$ and the item difficulty $\beta_i$.
What is new is the parameter $\alpha_i$, the item discrimination.
Its index $i$ shows that it is item-specific, so each item can have its own discrimination.
This is also the difference from the 1PL model in @eq-1PL, where $\alpha$ has no index because it is the same for all items.

The Rasch model is thus a special case of the 2PL in which the discrimination of every item equals 1:

$$
\alpha_i = 1 \quad \text{for all } i \in \{1, \ldots, I\}
$$

We can see this in the output of the Rasch model (@lst-rasch-item-param): the column `a`, which contains the $\alpha_i$ values, equals 1 for all items.

### Interpretation of the Item Discrimination Parameter

As with the Rasch model, we can write the 2PL on the log-odds scale, where it becomes linear [@debelakIntroductionRaschModel2022]:

$$
\text{logit}(p_{ni}) = \alpha_i(\theta_n - \beta_i) = \alpha_i\theta_n - \alpha_i\beta_i
$$

If we define $\gamma_i = -\alpha_i\beta_i$, this becomes

$$
\text{logit}(p_{ni}) = \alpha_i\theta_n + \gamma_i = \gamma_i + \alpha_i\theta_n,
$$ {#eq-2PL-CFA}

where the second form follows the usual order of a regression equation: a straight line in $\theta_n$ with intercept $\gamma_i$ and slope $\alpha_i$.^[The intercept $\gamma_i$ is what `mirt` reports in the column `d` when `IRTpars = FALSE`. For the Rasch model, where $\alpha_i = 1$, it is simply $-\beta_i$, the item easiness described above.]

The item discrimination is thus the slope of the item's line on the log-odds scale, as we already saw in @fig-ICC_spec_obj_2PL_logodds.
On the probability scale, it determines how steep the ICC is (@fig-2PL-expl-discr): the larger $\alpha_i$, the steeper the ICC around the item difficulty $\beta_i$, and the smaller $\alpha_i$, the flatter it becomes.

::: {#fig-2PL-expl-discr}
